example. There are many living creatures which in the winter time are devoid of all life, but as soon as summer returns, natural heat begets new life, so that the body rises again in that substance in which it previously exerted vital motion, just as an herb, which dies in winter, revives again in spring. The death of these things is therefore judged to be natural, but the restoration of the new life, through its own knowledge, is supernatural. But because man becomes accustomed to all these things, the smallest part, which should be further weighed and examined, is approached, while both natural and supernatural things are neglected.

Many also are wont to pass over without reflection the disposition of man, which is itself also supernatural; likewise those monsters which bring stains into the world together; all these things are indeed natural but, through an arisen imagination, show themselves supernaturally, which supernatural form and disposition the mother accidentally impressed upon the infant through her own imagination, just as we often see that some men have certain habits instilled in them, which they can never renounce, however much they labor to become unaccustomed to them. Such an instilled force of man is natural; the conception in the mother's womb, which the imagination of these things provided, is supernatural, and subject to those things which the heaven impressed.

Finally, neither do I deny this, that no one can support what exceeds the forces of nature with unshaken foundations and indubitable causes, to avoid injury to the truth, unless those things are first known which become known to us from their physical course, for they have drawn their birth and their form from supernatural things;

